# Museum: classic synth sounds rebuilt from their published settings, each converted to this
# engine's units and admitted by the user in a side-by-side against a reference recording
# (decisions D88, D103, D104, D107). No effects: the sounds are as the instrument made them.
# Candidates wait in resources/museum-candidates, outside the plugin.

[DX7 E.PIANO 1]
room = Museum
# The DX7's electric piano (ROM 1A, voice 11): algorithm 5, three pairs of a sine carrier and a
# sine modulator. Two pairs at 1:1 are the body, a fifth of a hertz apart, one with feedback:
# their brightness is the bark that follows how hard the key is hit. The third pair's modulator runs
# at fourteen times the note and is the tine: a bell that drops 18 dB in a seventh of a second
# and then fades. Ratios, detunes, indices (5.5, 2.3 and 0.38 radians at velocity 99), decay
# rates (4 dB a second at middle C, faster going up) and velocity laws are the voice's own
# numbers converted. Two things are taken from a recording of the voice instead: the feedback
# amount, and what a detune step is. The two open DX7 emulations disagree on it by a factor of
# five; at the larger figure the upper harmonics beat ten times a second and buzz.
g1 = FM4 algo=twoPairs ratio2=1 ratio3=0.999134 ratio4=1.000867 level2=0 level3=1 level4=0 fb=0.6 level=0.8
g2 = FM2 ratio=14 index=0 carrier=1 level=0.4
p1 = Off
env1 = attack=1 decay=17200 sustain=0 release=530 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 velocity=0.6 keyTime=0.38
env2 = attack=1 decay=530 sustain=0 release=100 decayCurve=-1 keyTime=0.38
env3 = attack=1 decay=2860 sustain=0 release=100 decayCurve=-1 keyTime=0.38
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level2 amount=1 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level2 amount=0.2564 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level2 amount=-0.104 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level4 amount=0.8142 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env1 dst=g1.level4 amount=-0.0674 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.index amount=0.2036 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.index amount=-0.0344 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env3 dst=g2.index amount=0.1122 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env3 dst=g2.index amount=-0.0189 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=key dst=g1.ratio3 amount=0.000806
mod = src=key dst=g1.ratio4 amount=-0.000806
voice = env2ToP1=0
master = -2.5

[DX7 BASS 1]
room = Museum
# The DX7's bass (ROM 1A, voice 15): algorithm 16, one sine carrier with three modulators. It
# sounds two octaves under the key, as on the instrument (the voice is transposed an octave
# down and its carrier runs at half the note). A modulator at the carrier's pitch (2.5 radians,
# fading a little faster than the note) makes the round body. A second one is itself modulated
# at ten times the pitch (3.9 radians, gone in a second): the bark at the start of each note,
# stronger the harder the key is hit. A third, with a modulator at eighteen times the pitch and
# full feedback, is a click that only the upper keys have. Ratios, indices, decay rates, rate
# scaling and velocity laws are the voice's own numbers converted; nothing is fitted. Against a
# recording of the voice (Hexter, at key velocity 100) the first sixteen harmonics are within
# 1.3 dB in the middle, over seven keys and two seconds.
g1 = FM2 ratio=9 carrier=0.5 index=0 feedback=1 oct=-1 level=0
g2 = FM4 algo=stackPlusOne ratio2=1 ratio3=1 ratio4=10 level2=0 level3=0 level4=0 fb=0 combine=fm amount=0 oct=-2 level=0.8
p1 = Off
env1 = attack=1 decay=13724 sustain=0 release=134 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 velocity=0 keyTime=0.875
env2 = attack=1 decay=2451 sustain=0 release=100 decayCurve=-1 keyTime=0.69
env3 = attack=1 decay=536 sustain=0 release=100 decayCurve=-1 keyTime=0.125
mod = src=env1 dst=g2.level2 amount=0.2812 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env1 dst=g2.level2 amount=0.2812
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.level3 amount=0.2275 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.level3 amount=0.1145 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.level4 amount=0.8517 via=velocity
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.level4 amount=0.0302
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.index amount=0.7767 via=velocity curve=logarithmic
mod = src=env3 dst=g1.index amount=-0.1252 curve=logarithmic
mod = src=key dst=g2.amount amount=0.8
voice = env2ToP1=0
master = -3.5

[Prophet-5 Sync II]
room = Museum
# The Prophet-5's sync lead: factory program 3-2, "Sync II", the sound of The Cars' "Let's Go".
# Oscillator B, silent, is the master; oscillator A, a pulse near square, is locked to it and
# pulled up by the filter envelope through Poly-Mod: two octaves above at the envelope's peak,
# falling over a second to a third of an octave. That falling is the sweep. No resonance; the
# same envelope opens the filter; the amplifier is a plain gate; five voices. The wheel adds a
# vibrato to the master, as the program's Wheel-Mod does.
# From the instrument's own data (the 2020 Prophet-5's factory bank holds the original forty
# programs as numbers; the 1978 patch sheet agrees but for one knob) and the service manual's
# calibration: oscillator pitches, the pulse width (52 %; fitted 53.5), the Poly-Mod depth
# (1.97 octaves; fitted 2.04), envelope sustain (0.16 to 0.23; fitted 0.15) and attack (53 ms;
# fitted 40). Fitted to a recording of a real Rev 3 playing this program, where units differ
# and the documents disagree: the filter envelope's reach (4.1 octaves: the patch sheet gives
# 3.2, the stored data 4.9), the cutoff (0.7 octave above the nominal), and the decay time
# (1.2 s time constant: 1.7 nominal, and real units run faster).
g1 = VA shape=saw level=0
g2 = VA shape=pulse pw=0.535 combine=sync level=0.5
p1 = Ladder cutoff=756 res=0 key=1 drive=0 bass=0
env1 = attack=1 decay=100 sustain=1 release=4 velocity=0
env2 = attack=40 decay=9900 sustain=0.15 release=80 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1
lfo1 = shape=triangle rate=6 retrigger=off
mod = src=env2 dst=g2.pitch amount=0.51
mod = src=lfo1 dst=g1.pitch amount=0.008 via=wheel
voice = env2ToP1=0.41 velToP1=0 drift=1
play = mode=poly voices=5
master = -9

[Prophet-5 Brass]
room = Museum
# The Prophet-5's brass: factory program 1-1, the first sound the instrument plays. One
# sawtooth oscillator at full level (oscillator B is set up but switched off), no resonance,
# five voices. The sound is its filter: closed under the note when a key goes down, it opens
# over five and a half octaves in a twentieth of a second and settles a good part of the way
# back within a few tenths: a bright blare that mellows as the chord is held. The wheel adds a
# vibrato of about five a second, as the player of the reference recording does on long chords.
# From Sequential's own factory data and the service manual's calibration: the oscillator, the
# filter's reach (5.6 octaves: the fit to the recording left it exactly there), the envelopes'
# shapes. Fitted to five chords of a real Rev 3 playing this program, where units differ: the
# cutoff (0.2 octave above nominal), the filter's opening and settling times (0.6 of nominal)
# and sustain, the amplifier's decay and sustain. A first version, built from the documents
# alone with two values borrowed from Sync II, sounded "like a midi pc sound card" to the user.
g1 = VA shape=saw level=0.8
p1 = Ladder cutoff=105 res=0 key=1 drive=0 bass=0
env1 = attack=17 decay=3000 sustain=0.63 release=704 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 velocity=0
env2 = attack=49 decay=1440 sustain=0.4 release=392 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1
lfo1 = shape=triangle rate=5 retrigger=off
mod = src=lfo1 dst=pitch amount=0.008 via=wheel
voice = env2ToP1=0.56 velToP1=0 drift=1
play = mode=poly voices=5
master = -1.5

[Minimoog Fat Bass]
room = Museum
# "Emerson's Fat Bass from Brain Salad Surgery", from Tom Rhea's Sound Charts for the Minimoog
# (Moog Music, 1974, page 15). The chart: three sawtooth oscillators at full level, one at 32'
# and two at 16', tuned to unison; filter cutoff -2, emphasis 5, amount of contour 3.5, both
# keyboard-control switches on; filter contour attack 0, decay 300 msec, sustain 0; loudness
# contour attack 0, decay 600 msec, sustain 5; decay switch on, no glide. Play it an octave or
# two under middle C: the key is the 8' pitch, as on the instrument. One voice; a note joined
# to the one before it does not start the filter's sweep again, a note struck anew does, and
# that sweep at every struck note is the sound.
# The chart also has the filter-modulation switch on with the wheel a little raised: oscillator
# 3 shakes the cutoff at its own pitch, a growl more than a wobble. That is the ladder's FM
# here, four tenths of an octave either way.
# From the chart and the service manual: the oscillators, and the cutoff (-2 on the dial is
# about 250 Hz at the lowest key and follows the keyboard: 752 Hz at middle C by the manual;
# fitted 900). What the documents do not pin down is fitted to a recording of a real Model D
# set to this chart, played with every note struck: how far the contour opens the filter (2.8
# octaves; the manuals say between 1.4 and 2.2), emphasis 5 (0.06: little, with three
# oscillators driving the filter), the contours' times (the legend is close to the time a
# contour takes to finish), sustain 5 (0.75 of full), and how far oscillator 3 shakes the
# filter (the chart only draws the wheel "a little up"; the top of the spectrum asks for 0.4
# of an octave).
# Still short of the recording: above 3 kHz the real one keeps a faint sizzle through the whole
# note, 50 to 60 dB under it, that the shake only half accounts for.
g1 = VA shape=saw oct=-2 level=0.8
g2 = VA shape=saw oct=-1 fine=3 level=0.8
g3 = VA shape=saw oct=-1 fine=-2 level=0.8
p1 = Ladder cutoff=902 res=0.06 key=1 drive=0 bass=0 fm=0.4
env1 = attack=1 decay=624 sustain=0.75 release=624 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1 velocity=0
env2 = attack=1 decay=450 sustain=0 release=450 decayCurve=-1 releaseCurve=-1
voice = env2ToP1=0.285 velToP1=0 drift=1
play = mode=legato priority=low
master = 3
